From 39423854cf34eef77ee4b08279dff2f0863700b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:44:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: absolute-deadline frame pacer

Passage's stock frame limiter is open-loop on a per-frame delta: it
measures frameTime, computes 1/lockedFrameRate - frameTime, sleeps that,
and discards the result. Nothing ever references an absolute timeline, so
every per-frame error is a permanent, unrecoverable loss.

That cost real fps on a 486DX2-66. The port was NOT compute-bound --
measured per-frame work is ~42.7ms against a 66.67ms budget, ~24ms/frame
of idle slack -- yet it measured 13.99fps. The gap was entirely pacing.

Root cause, confirmed three independent ways: SDL_SYS_DelayNS calls
DJGPP's delay(1) whenever >1ms remains, that call actually takes ~1.236ms
(measured on this hardware by tests/probes/dlygran.c), and the loop can
only re-check its exit condition BETWEEN calls -- so every sleep lands up
to a full quantum late. Instrumentation put the pacer 1.21ms past its
deadline every frame, against the 1.148ms/frame the fps gap works out to.

This replaces it with an absolute deadline that advances by exactly one
tick per frame, so whatever a frame overspends the next repays:

- Converge on the deadline rather than commit to one sleep (doskutsu's
  loop-pacer pattern): halve the remainder and re-measure, since no single
  sleep can land accurately.
- Final approach via SDL_Delay(0) -- pure DOS_Yield, ~0.25ms granularity,
  5x finer than delay(1)'s quantum -- exiting at half a quantum so the
  landing is symmetric about the deadline rather than always late. A
  busy-wait was tried here and REVERTED: it starved the BIOS timer, and
  because Passage's own fps report divides by time(NULL), the reported
  number IMPROVED while the real rate collapsed.
- Debt clamp at 5 ticks, matching doskutsu's MAX_CATCHUP. Resyncs FORGIVE
  debt, and forgiven time can never be earned back under a hard cap.
- Every delta signed with a sanity ceiling: uclock() is not monotonic on
  real DOS hardware (~1 corrupt sample per 1800 frames, measured).
- The audio pump may spend the pacer's idle slack on ring refills instead
  of sleeping through it, bounded three ways so it cannot become the
  unbounded burst patch 0008 was written to prevent.

Real hardware, 486DX2-66 + Cirrus CL-GD5434: 13.992 -> 14.936fps, with
steady-state at 14.9989fps against the 15.000fps hard ceiling
lockedFrameRate=15 imposes. Remaining gap is 3-4 discrete stall frames
per run, under separate investigation.

Deliberately NOT included: a shortened deadline to compensate for those
stalls. It was tried and reverted -- steady-state duly rose to 15.089fps,
but tightening the deadline also converts more frames into missed frames,
stall cost more than doubled, and the run average did not move. You
cannot compensate your way past stalls by tightening the target.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 324 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 323 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index 18737f0..3a56a49 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -946,7 +946,80 @@ char playGame() {
     
 
     double lastFrameTimeStamp = Time::getCurrentTime();
-    
+
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // DOS-PORT: absolute-deadline frame pacer state. See the pacer itself,
+    // below, for why the stock delta pacer cannot hit 15fps on this
+    // hardware no matter how accurate the sleep underneath it is.
+    //
+    // Paced entirely in SDL's own timebase (SDL_GetTicksNS), never
+    // minorGems' Time::getCurrentTime() -- that is a separate,
+    // millisecond-truncated gettimeofday() clock, and mixing the two is
+    // its own error source independent of the pacer's shape. Sibling port
+    // doskutsu paces exclusively through SDL's timer API for the same
+    // reason.
+    // The pacer targets exactly 1/lockedFrameRate. A deliberately-shortened
+    // deadline was tried on real hardware and REVERTED -- recorded here
+    // because the result is counterintuitive and someone will otherwise try
+    // it again.
+    //
+    // The reasoning was sound and the mechanism worked exactly as intended:
+    // lockedFrameRate=15 makes 15.000fps an unreachable ceiling (the deadline
+    // advances one tick per frame, so a stalled frame is a permanent loss no
+    // later frame may repay), so shortening the target by the measured mean
+    // excess (0.293ms/frame across two clean A/B runs) should have landed the
+    // average on 15. With a 0.40ms compensation, steady-state duly rose from
+    // 14.9989fps to **15.089fps** -- above 15, precisely as predicted, with
+    // the pacer still landing on the shortened deadline to within 0.03ms.
+    //
+    // The run average did not move: 14.936 -> 14.942fps. Because tightening
+    // the deadline ALSO converts more frames into missed frames, and every
+    // miss is a loss. Stall cost more than doubled, 0.279 -> 0.654 ms/frame
+    // (w1 behind_repay 44->54; w7's worst single frame 350-380ms -> 528ms),
+    // consuming exactly what the compensation bought.
+    //
+    // The lesson, which generalises past this port: **you cannot compensate
+    // your way past stalls by tightening the target.** Stalls are the binding
+    // constraint; squeezing the budget just manufactures more of them. Fix
+    // the stalls or accept the average. A knob that buys +0.006fps -- deep
+    // inside run-to-run noise -- while making the underlying behaviour worse
+    // has no business in the tree, however good its rationale looked.
+    const Uint64 dosFrameTickNS = (Uint64)( SDL_NS_PER_SECOND / lockedFrameRate );
+
+    // Resync rather than sprint if we ever fall this far behind. doskutsu's
+    // own catch-up history (its patches 0281/0282/0286/0288) is a series of
+    // real incidents where an unclamped backlog manufactured work that
+    // should not have existed -- once from an unsigned-subtraction
+    // wraparound that turned a small delta into "tens of days". dossage
+    // cannot manufacture ticks (one loop iteration IS one world step here,
+    // dX += timeDelta), so the failure mode is instead a visible speed-up
+    // as the pacer stops sleeping to repay impossible debt. Either way the
+    // clamp is not optional: the known ~57ms first-flush stall alone would
+    // otherwise be repaid as a sprint.
+    // 5 ticks, matching doskutsu's own MAX_CATCHUP. This was 2, and that was
+    // too tight: real-hardware PACEDBG shows 4 resync events inside a single
+    // 300-frame window (plus 8 more during startup), and a resync FORGIVES
+    // the outstanding debt -- that time is permanently lost, and since
+    // lockedFrameRate=15 makes 15.000fps a hard ceiling the game may approach
+    // but never exceed, forgiven time can never be earned back. Every such
+    // event pushes the measured average further below 15 for the rest of the
+    // run.
+    //
+    // Why a larger clamp is safe HERE, where doskutsu needed the guard:
+    // doskutsu's catch-up loop manufactures simulation ticks, so an inflated
+    // backlog creates work that should not exist -- that is the spiral its
+    // patches 0281/0282/0286/0288 were written against. dossage cannot do
+    // that. One loop iteration IS one world step (dX += timeDelta), so
+    // "catching up" here only means not sleeping: the frame runs at its
+    // natural ~42.7ms against a 66.67ms budget, i.e. ~24ms/frame of repayment
+    // capacity. 5 ticks (333ms) of debt is therefore repayable in ~14 frames
+    // of ordinary running, bounded and self-limiting, with the clamp still
+    // there to stop an unbounded sprint if work ever genuinely exceeds budget.
+    const Uint64 dosFrameMaxDebtNS = 5 * dosFrameTickNS;
+
+    Uint64 dosNextFrameDeadlineNS = SDL_GetTicksNS() + dosFrameTickNS;
+#endif
+
     // use to slow player motion after spouse has died
     char movingThisFrame = true;
     
@@ -1075,6 +1148,7 @@ char playGame() {
 
 
     while( !done ) {
+
         
         if( getKeyDown( SDL_SCANCODE_S ) ) {
             stepDX = false;
@@ -1398,11 +1472,67 @@ char playGame() {
             // DOS-PORT: pump-loop-cost timing (was here through patch
             // 0026) served its purpose -- see presentScreen()'s own
             // comment above for why it's removed now.
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+            // DOS-PORT: spend the frame limiter's own idle slack on refilling
+            // the audio ring, instead of sleeping through it.
+            //
+            // Patch 0008 capped this loop at 4 iterations because an
+            // unbounded catch-up burst once cost a catastrophic 2fps, and
+            // that cap must stay as a floor. But 4 is far too few during a
+            // ring REFILL: real-hardware PACEDBG data (2720-frame run) shows
+            // two ~300-frame windows where per-frame work jumps from ~42.7ms
+            // to 52-58ms with 31-33 frames going over budget -- window 1
+            // (t=0-20s, the initial fill) and window 7 (t=122-142s). SONG.WAV
+            // is a 136-second loop, so window 7 is the loop restart. Those two
+            // windows alone cost ~0.91ms/frame averaged across the whole run,
+            // which is 10x the pacer's own residual and the single largest
+            // remaining term in the fps number.
+            //
+            // The fix uses what this whole investigation turned up: the game
+            // does ~42.7ms of work against a 66.67ms budget, so there is
+            // ~24ms/frame of slack the pacer was about to sleep away. Pump
+            // while the ring is short AND the deadline is still comfortably
+            // ahead, so a refill burst lands in idle time instead of pushing
+            // frames over budget. The deadline is the honest budget here --
+            // the loop cannot run long without the pacer noticing, because it
+            // is checking the very clock the pacer paces on.
+            //
+            // Bounded three ways, deliberately: the 0008 cap still applies
+            // unconditionally for the first 4 iterations, extra iterations
+            // require real measured slack, and a hard ceiling matches the
+            // backend's own >32-calls runaway guard (SDL/0067) so this can
+            // never become the unbounded burst 0008 was written to prevent.
+            const int pumpHardCap = 32;
+            const Sint64 pumpSlackMarginNS = 8000000;   // 8ms of headroom
+
+            while( pumpIters < pumpHardCap &&
+                   SDL_DOSAudioRingFillMs() < pumpClampedTargetMs ) {
+
+                if( pumpIters >= pumpMaxIters ) {
+                    // Past the 0008 cap: only continue on measured slack.
+                    const Sint64 pumpLeftNS =
+                        (Sint64)dosNextFrameDeadlineNS - (Sint64)SDL_GetTicksNS();
+                    if( pumpLeftNS < pumpSlackMarginNS ) {
+                        break;
+                        }
+                    // Wraparound guard, same rule as the pacer below.
+                    if( pumpLeftNS > (Sint64)dosFrameTickNS ) {
+                        break;
+                        }
+                    }
+
+                if( !SDL_DOSAudioPump() ) {
+                    break;
+                    }
+                pumpIters ++;
+                }
+#else
             while( pumpIters < pumpMaxIters &&
                    SDL_DOSAudioRingFillMs() < pumpClampedTargetMs &&
                    SDL_DOSAudioPump() ) {
                 pumpIters ++;
                 }
+#endif
             }
 #endif
 
@@ -1440,12 +1570,204 @@ char playGame() {
         // call, that machine's own measured fps (12.41-13.25) never
         // clears the 15fps budget, so extraTime was negative on every
         // frame and SDL_Delay was never reached during gameplay at all.
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+        // DOS-PORT: absolute-deadline frame pacer, replacing the stock
+        // per-frame delta sleep above (extraTime is left computed because
+        // nothing else here should change shape; it is simply not what we
+        // sleep on).
+        //
+        // WHY: the stock pacer is open-loop. It measures frameTime,
+        // computes 1/15 - frameTime, sleeps that, and discards the result.
+        // Nothing ever references an absolute timeline, so every per-frame
+        // error is a permanent, unrecoverable loss. Three real-hardware
+        // rounds on a 486DX2-66 measured those losses directly (see
+        // docs/PLAN.md's "15fps plan"): ~1.0ms/frame of sleep overshoot,
+        // ~2.8ms/frame of over-requested target (frameTime and the sleep
+        // request are BOTH truncated to whole milliseconds -- by
+        // TimeDOS.cpp's tv_usec/1000 and by (Uint32)(extraTime*1000)
+        // respectively -- on a machine whose clock is good to ~0.84us),
+        // and ~0.85ms/frame amortized from a one-time startup stall.
+        // Together ~4.65ms/frame against the 4.83ms/frame actually
+        // missing: essentially the entire gap between the measured
+        // 13.99fps and the 15fps target.
+        //
+        // Critically, this is NOT a performance problem. The same round
+        // measured real per-frame game work at only ~42.5ms against the
+        // 66.67ms budget -- ~24ms/frame of idle slack. The machine is not
+        // struggling; the pacer is leaking. (Sibling port doskutsu, a far
+        // heavier engine, clears 15fps on this identical hardware.)
+        //
+        // An absolute deadline fixes all three losses at once without
+        // needing to know their causes: whatever a frame overspends is
+        // repaid by the next frame sleeping correspondingly less, so no
+        // single sleep's precision matters. That is why the earlier
+        // -3ms fixed-compensation experiment failed (13.84fps, within
+        // noise) -- a constant offset cannot correct a variable error.
+        {
+            const Uint64 nowNS = SDL_GetTicksNS();
+
+
+            // Signed delta deliberately: an unsigned wraparound here is a
+            // real, real-hardware-confirmed hazard on this backend, not a
+            // theoretical one -- uclock() (SDL's DOS timebase) is not
+            // monotonic, and the SDL/0137 diagnostic caught it live at
+            // roughly 1 sample per 1800 frames. doskutsu's own patch 0288
+            // was the same class of bug: an unsigned subtraction turned a
+            // small delta into "tens of days" and blew through every
+            // downstream guard. Treat anything implausible as a lost
+            // measurement and resync rather than trust it.
+            const Sint64 remainingNS = (Sint64)dosNextFrameDeadlineNS - (Sint64)nowNS;
+
+            if( remainingNS > (Sint64)dosFrameTickNS ) {
+                // Ahead by more than a whole frame: the clock lied (or the
+                // deadline is stale). Resync; never sleep on a bad reading.
+                dosNextFrameDeadlineNS = nowNS + dosFrameTickNS;
+                SDL_Delay( 0 );
+                }
+            else if( remainingNS > 0 ) {
+                // Converge on the deadline rather than commit to one sleep.
+                //
+                // The first version of this pacer slept the whole remainder
+                // in a single SDL_DelayNS and trusted the additive deadline
+                // to repay any overshoot on the following frame. On real
+                // hardware that recovered most of the gap (13.99 -> 14.73fps)
+                // but left ~0.53ms/frame on the table: the sleep lands ~1.1ms
+                // past the deadline every frame, and repayment measurably did
+                // not keep up with it.
+                //
+                // doskutsu's loop pacer solves this by sleeping only HALF the
+                // estimated remainder and re-measuring against the real clock
+                // each pass, so it approaches the deadline instead of
+                // overshooting it once and correcting afterwards. Each pass
+                // costs one more DOS_Yield() (~0.25ms measured, and it feeds
+                // the cooperative audio scheduler, so the calls are not
+                // wasted), and dossage has ~24ms/frame of slack to spend on
+                // them.
+                //
+                // Sub-millisecond resolution throughout: SDL_DelayNS routes
+                // straight to this backend's SDL_SYS_DelayNS with no
+                // truncation to whole milliseconds, and still runs the
+                // DOS_Yield() the audio thread depends on (see the SDL_Delay
+                // note above -- that requirement is unchanged).
+                Sint64 leftNS = remainingNS;
+
+                // Hand off from sleeping to spinning while there is still
+                // more than one sleep-quantum of room left.
+                //
+                // WHY THIS THRESHOLD IS NOT SMALL: SDL_SYS_DelayNS cannot
+                // land accurately no matter what it is asked for. Its inner
+                // loop calls DJGPP's delay(1) whenever >1ms remains, that
+                // call really takes ~1.236ms (measured on this hardware by
+                // tests/probes/dlygran.c), and the loop can only re-check
+                // its exit condition BETWEEN calls -- so any sleep can
+                // finish up to roughly one full quantum past what was asked.
+                // Halving does not fix this: the last halving still ends
+                // with a sleep, and that sleep still overshoots. Measured
+                // directly (PACEDBG.LOG, 300-frame window): a 6-iteration
+                // convergence loop with a 0.25ms floor still exited an
+                // average of 1.178ms PAST the deadline, against the
+                // 1.148ms/frame the real-hardware fps gap works out to.
+                // That is the whole remaining gap, and it is why neither
+                // the additive deadline nor the halving recovered it.
+                const Sint64 dosFrameSpinHandoffNS = 3000000;   // 3ms
+
+                while( leftNS > dosFrameSpinHandoffNS ) {
+                    SDL_DelayNS( (Uint64)( leftNS / 2 ) );
+
+                    const Uint64 afterNS = SDL_GetTicksNS();
+                    leftNS = (Sint64)dosNextFrameDeadlineNS - (Sint64)afterNS;
+
+                    // Same wraparound guard as above: a bad clock reading
+                    // must never turn into a long sleep or a spin.
+                    if( leftNS > (Sint64)dosFrameTickNS ) {
+                        break;
+                        }
+                    }
+
+                // Final approach: SDL_Delay(0) in a loop -- a pure
+                // DOS_Yield() with no delay(1) anywhere near it.
+                //
+                // A previous revision of this block busy-waited on the clock
+                // instead, on the reasoning that a sub-3ms non-yielding gap
+                // was nothing against the ~150ms audio ring depth. That was
+                // wrong, and real hardware punished it twice over:
+                //   1. It starved the BIOS timer interrupt. time(NULL)
+                //      advanced only 41s across a ~90s run -- 54% of ticks
+                //      simply lost -- which also made the engine's own fps
+                //      report meaningless, since netTime is time(NULL)-based.
+                //   2. Per-frame work GREW ~15.7ms (42.8 -> 58.5ms, read off
+                //      entry_err). Not yielding starves the cooperative audio
+                //      scheduler, so SDL_DOSAudioPump() has far more to do
+                //      every frame. 3ms/frame of withheld yield is ~45ms of
+                //      every second, not a rounding error.
+                // Real fps was ~6.5, not the 14.3 the broken report claimed.
+                // Recorded here because the mistake is not obvious from the
+                // code and the fps number actively lied about it.
+                //
+                // SDL_Delay(0) takes SDL_SYS_DelayNS's ns==0 early return,
+                // which is DOS_Yield() and nothing else: ~0.25ms granularity
+                // (measured), 5x finer than delay(1)'s ~1.236ms quantum,
+                // while still feeding the audio thread and letting interrupts
+                // run.
+                //
+                // Exit at HALF a quantum rather than at zero, so the landing
+                // is symmetric about the deadline -- sometimes a hair early,
+                // sometimes a hair late, averaging out -- instead of always
+                // overshooting. Stopping at zero would put the mean landing a
+                // full half-quantum late again, which is the same shape of
+                // bug in a smaller size.
+                // Tuned against real hardware, not assumed. At 0.125ms (half
+                // the measured ~0.25ms DOS_Yield quantum) the landing came in
+                // at exit_err +0.053..+0.092ms across five clean steady-state
+                // windows -- consistently a hair LATE, where DOSBox had shown
+                // it a hair early. The real quantum here is evidently a little
+                // coarser than DOSBox's, so exit half of the measured residual
+                // earlier to centre the landing on the deadline rather than
+                // just past it. Small, but it is a systematic bias applied to
+                // every frame, and the whole gap being chased is ~1ms.
+                const Sint64 dosFrameYieldHalfQuantumNS = 200000;   // 0.2ms
+
+                while( leftNS > dosFrameYieldHalfQuantumNS ) {
+                    SDL_Delay( 0 );
+
+                    const Uint64 afterNS = SDL_GetTicksNS();
+                    leftNS = (Sint64)dosNextFrameDeadlineNS - (Sint64)afterNS;
+
+                    // Wraparound guard, same rule as everywhere else here.
+                    if( leftNS > (Sint64)dosFrameTickNS ) {
+                        break;
+                        }
+                    }
+
+                dosNextFrameDeadlineNS += dosFrameTickNS;
+                }
+            else if( -remainingNS > (Sint64)dosFrameMaxDebtNS ) {
+                // Too far behind to repay honestly. Resync instead of
+                // sprinting: dossage cannot manufacture world steps to
+                // catch up (one loop iteration IS one step, dX +=
+                // timeDelta), so repaying a large debt would show up as a
+                // visible speed-up rather than dropped frames. The known
+                // ~57ms first-flush stall lands here exactly once per run.
+                dosNextFrameDeadlineNS = nowNS + dosFrameTickNS;
+                SDL_Delay( 0 );
+                }
+            else {
+                // Behind, but within the repayable window: skip the sleep
+                // and let the deadline advance normally, so the next frame
+                // naturally sleeps less and the timeline recovers.
+                dosNextFrameDeadlineNS += dosFrameTickNS;
+                SDL_Delay( 0 );
+                }
+
+            }
+#else
         if( extraTime > 0 ) {
             SDL_Delay( (Uint32)( extraTime * 1000 ) );
             }
         else {
             SDL_Delay( 0 );
             }
+#endif
 
         // start timing next frame
         lastFrameTimeStamp = Time::getCurrentTime();
-- 
2.47.3

